Hey! 

As this is their template - let’s make the following proposition (please correct the grammar if it’s way off).

[~accountid:712020:a7a7d4bc-10ca-44a0-a22e-fa8752330abd] 

{quote}we need to make sure that we prevent unauthorized access from happening.{quote}

We should maybe check with Tech Support first, if the user of the Estonian phone number could gain access to the account of the user (with Austrian phone number) now. And also if there is any chance of the accounts merging in the future. 
Because I am not sure if deleting the recently created Estonian account, will solve the main issue for the Austrian user. And the main issue is that the Estonian user could access the payment information/methods of the Austrian user, amongst other things. But we have not seen any further evidence, that the Estonian user used this access to defraud the Austrian user, after they (the Estonian user) had created a new account.

As far as I understand it, as soon as the phone number was changed from the Estonian one to the Austrian one, the Estonian user lost the access to the account. And then they created their own account.

{quote}This means that we need to delete one of the accounts (and ask the user to re-register) and make sure that the other account verifies their email address. Would that be doable?{quote}

In principle, it is doable for CS. We can manually delete the account after having sent the user and in-app message and/or email to inform them about the situation. Both accounts also have their emails addresses already verified.



{quote}Would you have any additional suggestions as to how to prevent the accounts from merging? Any experience from the CS side?{quote}

At least, I don’t have any experience with such cases, because phone number recycling + accessing the old user account should be a rather rare occurrence. According to my understanding, we don’t merge accounts at all. We only might archive them after a while and then create a new account if a user logs into it again. But besides being related in admin through comments and maybe the fraud graph, the old and the new account should not influence each other.

