Hi Andreas,

For now it is an isolated case, as it is not something the restaurants complain about weekly. The restaurant that complained is a small family business (husband and wife, only 2 employees from what they told me) that pretty much know the people around the restaurant's area and it is also easier for them to track how much discount is provided to the customers. Moreover, they were able to identify this situation, as the abusive eaters picked up the orders themselves (so who knows how many cases like this are happening with delivered orders). They have also informed me that this issue is not isolated as this situation occurred more than once, with multiple eaters. The only example I was able to get from them was the one Ioana left attached to this ticket (if you are missing the details I can re-attach them).

What I am trying to say is that I can definitely see this issue happening already with other restaurants, but due to (perhaps) a larger volume of orders and employees or pure lack of attention, they are unable to discover this abusive behaviour some users have. My point is that this might be something users do already but went unnoticed until now. Mostly, I think that it would be better to prevent rather than wait for other restaurants to start reporting it.

Regarding your reasonings, I have the following opinion:

1. {color:#333333}Most of the providers care about volumes and not financial efficiency of the discounts - Depends on the size of the providers within the market; for SMBs for example, every penny is important as they do not have the same volumes as other accounts (local heroes, mid-market, etc.). Also, for bigger restaurants with bigger volumes, a 10% difference in discount, in the bigger scheme of things really do matter.{color}
{color:#333333}2.{color} {color:#333333}We haven’t heard any case of bigger provider discounts abuse (such as food resale schemes). Except for deliberate{color} cherrypicking{color:#333333} behaviours such as this case. - So there were other instances already like this one? Maybe this trend will get bigger in the future and especially with SMBs. At some point our smaller providers might start noticing it as we expand our business to other cities.{color}3. {color:#333333}Some restaurants (I don't know if its majority or minority who do this) already artificially increase their prices.{color} {color:#333333}So hypothesis was that they still operate at a profit even though providing big first-time-order discounts - It is also happening here in Romania; artificial prices with artificial discounts added is a thing indeed, I do agree. But here I have an argument. I do have SMBs that are honest and offer competitive prices (we are talking about 5%-10% higher prices in the app) and just for the sake of growing their customer base are willing to support a higher discount for new users. At some point some of them might be affected if this abusive method gets more popular among our eaters.{color}{color:#333333}{color}


{color:#333333}This is my view on the current situation and I hope what I said above makes sense. If not, I do want to have my opinion challenged.{color}{color:#333333}{color} 


{color:#333333}Thank you once again for your implication and support!{color}{color:#333333}{color}


{color:#333333}Have a lovely day,{color}
{color:#333333}Vlad{color}

