Hi [~accountid:629736189bc7150068ce0c0e] 

Thank you for raising a ticket with the legal team. 

I have looked into [~accountid:62e9498948e310e672955e85] document outlining the reason for the dismissal. After reading the document I can see that this matter has been investigated thoroughly and Bolt is confident that this driver failed the selfie verification check. 

Under the General Platform Terms for Drivers, drivers must adhere to the Acceptable Use Policy which is at clause 7 of the terms. 

# Acceptable Use Policy

a. This Section describes the Acceptable Use Policy *and applies when any Service Provider is online on the Service Provider App*.

b. {color:#ff5630}You must not and you must ensure that all other Service Providers using the Bolt Platform in association with your account must not{color}:

i.{color:#ff5630} tamper with any security related features{color};

ii. use the Bolt Platform if Bolt UK has temporarily restricted access or stopped Service Providers from using it;

iii. {color:#ff5630}modify, interfere with, disrupt or hack the Bolt Platform, including introducing any software viruses or use the Bolt Platform to commit any fraud against Customers, the Bolt Parties or any third-parties (like credit card issuer, holder, etc.){color};

*_in addition the Fraud Policy at section 3 states:_* 

# Prohibited Behaviour

Behaviour that will be considered by Bolt to be *Prohibited Behaviour* is set out below:

3.1. providing or allowing (including any attempt to provide or allow) any inaccurate, misleading, duplicate or false Information to the Bolt Parties, the Bolt Platform and/or any Users relating to the provision, carrying out, time, duration or location of any service requested or provided via the Bolt Platform;

3.2. {color:#ff5630}using or allowing (including any attempt to use or allow) any device to be used for the falsification and/or manipulation of Information provided to the Bolt Parties, the Bolt Platform and/or any User{color}s;

3.3. registering (including any attempt to register) as a User of the Bolt Platform using any inaccurate, misleading, duplicate or false Information;

3.4. allowing any person to use the Bolt Platform via personal login details that do not belong to them;

From a legal point of view we are permitted to block the driver from using the platform if the driver has breached our T&Cs and the Fraud Policy. Moreover, Bolt can defend any argument raised on the basis that we have obligations to regulators to ensure the identification of the driver is the driver on the platform. 

Provided we gave the driver the right to appeal the decision (I note above that the driver was given 30 days to appeal) and the team reviewed this appeal thoroughly. In addition to the right to appeal, the driver should have been informed of the reason that he has been blocked (this is the statement of reasons, which should have a reference to the relevant T&Cs section that Bolt is relying on to initiate the block) then we have adhered to our obligations in the T&Cs. 

Therefore, provided that we keep the information on file and are certain that if the driver did initiate proceedings against Bolt that we have tangible evidence supporting Bolts rationale (that the driver has tampered with the selfie verification feature), then I cannot see any issues with you responding to the union informing them that Bolt is upholding the decision. 

Happy to discuss further if needed. 

Thanks

